<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Negasi Krestos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1703</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Negasi Krestos was a Shewan noble claiming Solomonic descent who around 1682 established an autonomous lordship in northern Shewa, in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> emperors weakened. His successors, first called meridazmach and later negus, built Shewa into a powerful kingdom whose last independent ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, became emperor and founded modern Ethiopia.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In the 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-adal-war">Ethiopian–Adal War</a> devastated Christian Abyssinia, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo-migrations">Oromo migrations</a> then carried conquests into Shewa and other regions. Shewa, whose towns of Barara, Tegulet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a> had served emperors as capitals and whose monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-libanos">Debre Libanos</a> was a religious centre of national importance, was almost entirely lost to imperial authority by the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarsa-dengel">Sarsa Dengel</a>'s reign. The few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> families that survived took refuge in the mountains of Menz, where the subdivision of Agancha became dominant.</p><p>Negasi was born in the parish of Agancha in the Gera district of Menz. The official account of the later Shewan dynasty, told by Serta Wold, a councillor of Negus Sahle Selassie, made him a male-line descendant of the Solomonic emperors through abeto Yaqob, fourth son of Lebna Dengel. Other traditions collected about 1840 held that his father was a rich landowner of Menz and that his royal claim came through his mother, Senebeit, whose father, Ras Faris of Dair, had escaped into Menz with followers of Emperor Susenyos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a young man Negasi defeated neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> chiefs, among them Lalo and Mamo, and annexed the districts of Ajabar and Termaber to Agancha. He then proclaimed himself ruling prince of Shewa and overcame those who disputed it, including the powerful Gera. He founded the church of Kidane Mehret at his base in Agancha. In 1683 he defeated the Arsi Oromo south of Menz and founded the town of Ayne on the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>, where he built a church of St. Mary and began to allot land by the tradition of rim, or gassa.</p><p>After fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wollo">Wollo</a> and Yejju Oromo north of Menz, he subdued the Oromo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate">Ifat</a>, which replaced Menz as the centre of the Shewan lordship, and took further districts including Debdabo, Mengist and Makfud. He allied with the Muslim leaders of Ifat against the Oromo. The historian Pankhurst credits him with moving the capital of Shewa from Tegulet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debre-berhan">Debre Berhan</a>, where his stone palace was still visible when Rochet d'Hericourt visited in 1840.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Early in the 18th century Negasi travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a> to pay homage to Emperor Iyasu I and died there of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. According to Donald Levine he was buried at the church of Fit Abbo, where his grave and the field he camped on, Nagassi Meda, remain objects of historical interest. He had not obtained the title of meridazmach, which his son and heir Sebestyanos later adopted on his own authority. His death away from his domain plunged Shewa into disorder, and the territories he had conquered reverted to their original rulers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagasi Krestos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>While the Gondar emperors weakened, <b>Negasi Krestos</b>, a Shewan noble claiming Solomonic descent, established an autonomous lordship in northern Shewa around 1682.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=12.6,37.47&amp;year=1693" aria-label="Gondar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5579 1694 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Negasi Krestos"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6029 1964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6029 1964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=12.6,37.47&amp;year=1693">What was here in 1693? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
